ar the water's edge. This hole is being prepared to bury the bread for cooking.\n\n2.  **Placing the Pot:** In frames 6 and 7, she places a metal pot (which contains the bread dough) into the hole she dug. The pot is likely wrapped in cloth or foil for insulation.\n\n3.  **Covering the Pot:** In frames 8 and 9, she covers the pot with sand or dirt to insulate it and allow the geothermal heat from the earth to cook the bread slowly.\n\n4.  **Uncovering and Retrieving the Bread:** In frames 10, 11, and 12, she is seen uncovering the pot and retrieving it. The pot contains the cooked bread, which appears dark and dense, characteristic of Icelandic rye bread (rúgbrauð) baked this way.\n\n5.  **Final Check:** In frame 13, she is inspecting the area or the pot, possibly ensuring the bread is properly cooked or preparing to serve it.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe woman is engaged in the **traditional Icelandic practice of baking bread using geothermal heat**. She is digging a hole, placing a pot of dough into it, covering it with earth, and later retrieving the cooked bread. This activity takes place **outdoors, by a body of water in a geothermal area in Iceland**, not in a restaurant or a kitchen. The process is a unique cultural experience that leverages Iceland's natural geothermal resources."}], 'formatted_responses': ["\n=== Batch 0-13 (13 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/hROKtPqktO8/frames/frame_1440.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/hROKtPqktO8/frames/frame_1445.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/hROKtPqktO8/frames/frame_1450.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/hROKtPqktO8/frames/frame_1455.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/hROKtPqktO8/frames/frame_1460.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/hROKtPqktO8/frames/frame_1465.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/hROKtPqktO8/frames/frame_1470.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/hROKtPqktO8/frames/frame_1475.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_proc